WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.199
Every modern computer, when you get
right down to the bare metal,

00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:06.010
is doing basically the same sort of thing.

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:09.210
I’ve said before that computers are just
overgrown calculators,

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:13.710
but how do you go from a simple calculator
to playing video games,

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.839
sending stuff over the internet,

00:00:14.839 --> 00:00:18.650
or even decompressing and displaying
the millions of pixels in this video?

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:23.020
In short, what’s your computer
actually doing?

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.110
Behind me is a scaled-up version of a computer,

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.420
but we’re going to go much, much simpler.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.039
If you take apart your phone or PC,

00:00:33.039 --> 00:00:37.000
somewhere in the heart of it will a
Central Processing Unit, or CPU,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.600
connected to all the other devices
that make it work.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.300
Now, to show a really basic example,
we’re not going to use all those devices.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.909
The first one we are going to use
is the clock.

00:00:45.909 --> 00:00:46.909
With every tick of the clock,

00:00:46.909 --> 00:00:51.070
our CPU goes through a step in what’s called
the “Fetch-Execute” cycle,

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.989
or “Fetch-Decode-Execute”.

00:00:52.989 --> 00:00:54.100
This clock is slightly magic,

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.540
in that it ticks (click) every time (click)
I click my fingers. (click)

00:00:57.980 --> 00:00:58.660
(click)

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.100
In the CPU I’m going to have three registers.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.540
These are bits of fast storage where the CPU
holds values that it’s working on right now.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:08.250
These are: a register that keeps track of
our instruction cycle,

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.180
another that loads our instructions from memory,

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.250
and an Accumulator.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.010
The final thing we need in our simplified
computer is somewhere to keep the instructions

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.140
and any values that we end up calculating.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.620
That is RAM, Random Access Memory.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.540
We call it Random Access because it
doesn't matter when or in what order

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.210
the information is read or written.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.370
So: that is our computer.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.130
Let’s run a simple program.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.100
All it’s going to do is count up.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.470
The processor has three steps:
Fetch, Decode, Execute.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.360
It will just repeat those on a loop,

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.180
that’s the one thing that’s
actually built into it.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.960
So we need some instructions,
actually in memory,

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.090
so let’s load our program into RAM.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:48.390
The RAM is also used to store our answers, 
our outputs.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.950
In the real world,
these would all be stored in binary,

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:55.660
but let’s not overcomplicate things right now,
let's keep them human-readable.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.380
An instruction has two parts.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.060
The first part is the instruction itself.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.560
And the second part is usually
a memory address.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.660
On each clock tick, the CPU will do
one of three things:

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.200
It will fetch an instruction from
a memory address.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.780
It will decode that instruction.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.380
And it will execute the instruction.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.900
Round and round in a loop.
So it’s going to count up.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.230
We're going to begin with a number,

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.810
and add one to it, over and over again.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:18.460
(click) Fetch.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.010
One clock tick.
The Program Counter is set to 0,

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.300
so the CPU fetches the instruction at address
0 in the memory

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.700
and puts it into the instruction register.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.540
(click) Decode.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.519
The CPU decodes the instruction.

00:02:30.519 --> 00:02:31.930
The first part is the instruction,

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.340
and the second part is a location.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:36.459
In our case, the instruction is LOAD
and the address is 6.

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:40.659
So we will be loading the value in
address 6 into the accumulator.

00:02:40.659 --> 00:02:42.250
(click) Execute.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.810
The CPU executes this instruction.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.299
It takes the value at address 6,

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:47.540
and loads it into the accumulator.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.620
In this case the value is 1.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.549
(click) Fetch.

00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:52.659
The program counter is incremented,

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:56.150
and the CPU fetches the next instruction
in the next bit of the memory.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.400
(click) Decode.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.920
The CPU decodes the instruction.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.219
This time, it’s ADD,
and the address is 7.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.580
So we’ll be adding what’s at address 7
into what is already in the accumulator.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:07.560
(click) Execute.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.580
The CPU executes the instruction.
We add the value at address 7.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.020
In this case,
it's the value 1.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.659
1 + 1 is 2.

00:03:14.659 --> 00:03:16.549
(click) Fetch.

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:18.730
From the next memory location, number 2.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:20.150
(click) Decode.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:24.930
An instruction to STORE the value in the accumulator
into RAM, at address 6.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.370
(click) Execute.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.620
Now, notice that we are overwriting what’s
already there,

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.760
so address 6 now has 2 in it,
instead of 1.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.120
(click) Fetch.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.079
A new instruction: JUMP.

00:03:36.079 --> 00:03:37.079
With a jump,

00:03:37.079 --> 00:03:40.290
the next address we fetch from is the one
in this instruction.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:41.670
(click) Decode.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.019
So we’re going to jump to
address number 1.

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:45.739
(click) Execute.

00:03:45.739 --> 00:03:48.560
The Program Counter is now back at 1.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.290
The ability to jump, to loop, and to build
instructions recursively is one of the foundations

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:55.620
of computer science.
So: we're back up there.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.049
(click) Fetch from location 1.

00:03:58.049 --> 00:03:59.290
(click) Decode.

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:00.919
It's the ADD instruction again.

00:04:00.919 --> 00:04:02.230
(click) Execute.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.900
Our accumulator still contains the values
from before,

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.110
so: 2 + 1 = 3.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:08.930
(click) Fetch.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:10.750
(click) Decode.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:11.750
STORE again.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.450
(click) Execute.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:15.109
Storing it into location number 6.

00:04:15.109 --> 00:04:16.290
(click) Fetch.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:17.479
(click) Decode.

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:18.660
(click) Execute.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.930
And we jump again.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:25.930
(click) Fetch. (click) Decode. (click) Execute.
(click) Fetch. (click) Decode. (click) Execute.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.420
(continues clicking) We're in a loop,
and we’re counting upwards

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.669
by one on every sixth clock cycle.
(stops clicking)

00:04:31.669 --> 00:04:36.300
Our program, with these simple instructions,
doesn’t have a halt command,

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:37.840
or any way to interrupt it,

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.060
so it will just keep incrementing
that value by one (many fast clicks)

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:46.090
until the number becomes so large it can
no longer be held by the memory address.

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:49.840
How it breaks then… well, that’s a whole
other video. (stops clicking)

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.650
And my fingers are tired.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.259
This is a very fiddly way to program a computer.

00:04:54.259 --> 00:04:56.479
In theory, it can be 
but at this level,

00:04:56.479 --> 00:04:59.020
these instructions are just encoded
in raw binary data,

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:01.220
which is basically unreadable for humans.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.110
So we can convert that base 2 binary
to base 16, hexadecimal,

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.590
at that level we call it machine code.

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:11.300
The next step up from that is a symbolic language
called Assembly, which is a bit more readable,

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.130
but it’s still close to working at that
bare metal.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:18.490
The original "Prince of Persia" game was completely
programmed in assembly.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:20.810
That is almost unbelievable to me:

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:25.979
painstakingly figuring out each pixel of animation
and encoding it into something that the computer

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.340
almost understands directly.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.560
Programming like that is complex, and hard,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.479
and prone to the sort of human error that
introduces massive security problems.

00:05:34.479 --> 00:05:37.889
It is difficult to code and difficult to debug.

00:05:37.889 --> 00:05:40.320
So rather than dealing with the messiness,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.220
or, well, the pristine logic of machine code,

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:46.680
higher-level languages were developed as an
intermediary step.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.890
Those languages handle all of that memory
reading and writing for us,

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:54.060
so all we need to focus on is
what we want the computer to do.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.260
So, here’s my code:

00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:00.220
just the same instructions,
phrased a little bit differently,

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:01.879
phrased for humans to be able
to read.

00:06:01.879 --> 00:06:05.949
I specify a variable, X. I then write a function
that loops forever,

00:06:05.949 --> 00:06:09.939
and each passing through that loop
I increment X by 1.

00:06:09.939 --> 00:06:12.539
Once I've written that code, I then pass it
to a compiler,

00:06:12.539 --> 00:06:15.069
which turns it into that
original machine code for me.

00:06:15.069 --> 00:06:16.259
So when I run the program,

00:06:16.259 --> 00:06:19.130
it’s loaded into the computer's memory,
and executed.

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:23.560
If I want to run it on a completely different
type of computer, a Mac instead of a PC,

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.640
I can compile it for that CPU instead.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.278
But this still doesn’t answer the question
of how the computer is

00:06:29.278 --> 00:06:33.970
doing something as complex as
decompressing and displaying this video.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:35.220
The answer to that is:

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:36.220
speed.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:37.910
At the speed I was clicking,
at the end there,

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:42.590
we were executing one instruction every couple
of seconds on one thread, one bit of the system.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:48.120
A modern CPU executes billions of instructions
per second – gigahertz – on multiple threads.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.000
But at the heart of your PC, or your phone,

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.640
there is still just a ticking clock and 
a fetch-execute cycle.

